  "worked_solution": "First, we compute ${B}^{2}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{B}^{2} & =B\\cdot B \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\frac{1}{2} & -\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ 0 & -\\frac{1}{2} & \\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\frac{1}{2} & -\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ 0 & -\\frac{1}{2} & \\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\frac{1}{2} & -\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ 0 & -\\frac{1}{2} & \\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =B\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that ${B}^{2}=B,$ so $B$ is an idempotent matrix.\n\nWe can use this fact to compute ${B}^{4}$ quickly, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{B}^{4} & ={B}^{2}\\cdot {B}^{2} \\\\  & =B\\cdot B \\\\  & ={B}^{2} \\\\  & =B\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n{B}^{4}=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\frac{1}{2} & -\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ 0 & -\\frac{1}{2} & \\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}].\n$$",
